-**B12. The per-seat capacity number is a lifetime high-water mark that has never been bounded from above.** Added 2026-10-16T16:53+01:00. HIS LIST item 5 of the 7 smaller defects, verbatim: *"A capacity number shared identically by 4 paid models and untouched for 185 days."* His ruling on the list: *"Fix it all."* **MEASURED, and the measurement is the contribution rather than a fix.** `max_successful_prompt_chars` stands at 465,206 for CC2, ChatGPT, Codex and Gemini -- 4 of 5 paid seats -- against DeepSeek's 110,076, and it first entered a committed fingerprint on 2026-04-13, **186 days** before the repository's newest commit. Against each seat's own stored history window of 50 dispatches it sits **6.49x** above CC2's recent maximum, 6.64x above ChatGPT's, 6.50x above Codex's, 4.89x above Gemini's and 1.51x above DeepSeek's. `measured_attention_span` and `compression_threshold` repeat the same number on all 5 seats, so 3 fields carry 1 figure. **IT HAS NEVER BEEN BOUNDED FROM ABOVE:** `max_failed_context_chars` is 0 and `max_failed_prompt_chars` is unset on every paid seat, so the arm that would lower the number has never fired, and one large dispatch in one old run still sets the routing ceiling today. **A HYPOTHESIS DIED ON THE WAY, and it is recorded because it was wrong in an instructive way.** The first explanation tried was that every seat in a star round receives the SAME prompt, so the field would record the runner's largest prompt rather than any model's capacity. That was REFUTED by the seats' own histories: they are not identical, sharing only 7 to 17 of 48 distinct values, and their recent maxima are 70,045 to 95,045. The mechanism is simpler and worse -- a lifetime maximum that never decays. **WHY IT IS NOT FIXED HERE:** `bench/reference_runner_v3.py` already records the disposition, verbatim -- *"a ratchet that cannot climb ... Changing it alters routing for paid seats and is a design decision, not a repair."* **AND IT BEARS ON HIS COHERENCE RULING.** He said the budget *"exists so that models never get fed work that is larger than they have been demonstrably proven to be able to handle in any experiment"*. The mechanism that actually serves that purpose is this fingerprint ceiling, and it currently cannot detect overload at all, because it is 6.5x above recent experience and has no upper bound. **NEEDS HIS RULING.** Three options: decay the number toward the recent window, bound it by a measured failure, or leave it as a proven floor and stop calling it a capacity. Producer: `scripts/the_capacity_number_is_a_high_water_mark_2026-10-16.py`.
